# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Aphex — environment
#
#  Copy this file to `.env`. Everything below is already set to a working local
#  default, so:
#
#      cp .env.example .env && pnpm dev
#
#  is enough to boot: SQLite in a local file, uploads on disk, emails printed to
#  the terminal. No Docker, no external accounts, no migration step.
#
#  The file is in three parts:
#
#    1. REQUIRED       — one value to generate before your first run.
#    2. LOCAL DEFAULTS — already correct for `pnpm dev`; change when you deploy.
#    3. OPTIONAL       — commented out. Uncomment a block to turn that feature on.
#
#  Anything left commented out is genuinely off, and the app says so at boot
#  rather than half-working.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ═══ 1. REQUIRED ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

## Signs session cookies. Generate one now — do not ship the placeholder, and do
## not reuse it between environments:
##
##     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
##
AUTH_SECRET=

## The app's own public URL — protocol and host exactly, no trailing slash.
##
## This must match the address you actually load in the browser. Better Auth
## compares the two and declines on a mismatch, and the decline surfaces as a
## bare 404 from /api/auth/* with nothing in the log naming the cause — so the
## symptom is "sign-up does nothing", not an error message.
##
## Two ways it goes wrong on a fresh project:
##   - Port 5173 was busy, so Vite started on 5174 and this still says 5173.
##   - You deployed and left it pointing at localhost.
## Change BOTH lines below when either happens.
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:5173

## Origins allowed to make cross-origin auth requests (CSRF check). Defaults to
## AUTH_URL when unset. Setting it REPLACES that default rather than adding to
## it, so if you list another origin you must list your own here too.
AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173


# ═══ 2. LOCAL DEFAULTS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Each of these is what the app already does with no configuration. They are
# written out (not commented) so you can see the running state at a glance.

## ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## SQLite via libsql. The schema is pushed at startup, so there is nothing to
## run first. The file is gitignored.
APHEX_SQLITE_URL=file:.aphex/website.db

## ── Storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## Uploads land in ./uploads and are reachable only through /media/:id/:filename,
## which is the route that enforces access control. Keep them out of static/ —
## anything in there is served publicly and baked into the build. Switch to S3/R2
## in the OPTIONAL section below when you deploy — a container filesystem is not
## a place to keep the only copy of anyone's images.
##
## Staying on disk in a container? Then this has to point at a mounted volume, or
## every redeploy starts with an empty media library. The one-click deploy configs
## set it to /data/uploads alongside the SQLite file.
# APHEX_UPLOADS_DIR=/data/uploads

## ── Email ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## In dev, mail goes to Mailpit instead of the internet. If Mailpit is running,
## read it at http://localhost:8025; if it isn't, sends are skipped and logged.
## Password reset and invitation links are also printed to the server console,
## so you are never locked out waiting for an email.
##   docker run -d -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 axllent/mailpit

## ── Jobs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## Scheduled publishes and event consumers run in-process in dev. For production
## see APHEX_EMBEDDED_WORKER / APHEX_WORKER_SECRET in the OPTIONAL section.


# ═══ 3. OPTIONAL ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

## ── Postgres instead of SQLite ──────────────────────────────────────────────
## SQLite is a real option in production, not a downgrade — reach for Postgres
## when you want a managed/shared database or several app replicas. Migrations
## live in drizzle/ and run on boot; `pnpm db:migrate` runs them by hand.
# APHEX_DATABASE=postgres
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://root:my-secret-password@localhost:5432/local
## …or the parts, instead of DATABASE_URL:
# PG_HOST=localhost
# PG_PORT=5432
# PG_USER=root
# PG_PASSWORD=my-secret-password
# PG_DATABASE=local

## Turso (hosted libsql) — point the SQLite URL at it and add a token.
# APHEX_SQLITE_URL=libsql://your-db.turso.io
# DATABASE_AUTH_TOKEN=

## The schema is migrated/pushed on boot so `pnpm dev` just works. With several
## replicas, prefer migrating as a deploy step and turn this off (false/0/no/off).
# APHEX_DB_AUTO_MIGRATE=false

## ── S3-compatible storage (Cloudflare R2, AWS S3, MinIO) ────────────────────
## All four of the first values must be set together — with any of them missing
## the app stays on local disk. Set these before your first production upload:
## assets already written to disk do not move themselves.
# R2_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2_BUCKET=
# R2_PUBLIC_URL=https://pub-<hash>.r2.dev
## Custom CDN domain in front of the bucket (overrides R2_PUBLIC_URL for asset URLs).
# R2_CDN_URL=https://cdn.example.com

## ── Transactional email in production (Resend) ──────────────────────────────
## Leave commented until you have a real key. With no key, email is disabled and
## the app logs that at boot — a placeholder value instead builds a live client
## around a bad key, and every password reset then fails silently at send time.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
## Sender identity. Must be an address on a domain you have verified with Resend.
# APHEX_EMAIL_FROM=Acme <hello@example.com>

## ── Sign-up policy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## Out of the box the first person to sign up becomes super admin, which is what
## you want on your laptop and not on a public URL. Pick one of these before you
## deploy — see src/lib/server/auth/auth.config.ts.
##
## Only this address can claim the instance:
# APHEX_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=you@example.com
## …or require a one-time code, printed to the server log at first boot:
# APHEX_BOOTSTRAP_CLAIM_CODE=true

## Require a verified email address before sign-in. Off by default so the first
## signup works without any mail server. Turn it on in production.
# AUTH_REQUIRE_EMAIL_VERIFICATION=true

## After the first account, sign-up requires a pending invitation by default.
## Set false to allow public account registration. This does not grant access to
## an organization; an owner or super admin must still add the user.
# AUTH_INVITE_ONLY=false

## Which second factors the sign-in challenge offers: `totp` (authenticator app),
## `email` (a mailed code), or both. Both by default; email is dropped anyway when
## no email adapter is configured. Backup codes are always available.
# AUTH_TWO_FACTOR_METHODS=totp

## ── Background jobs in production ───────────────────────────────────────────
## In dev an in-process loop runs the queue. In production it is off by default, so
## unless you turn one of the two options below on, jobs are queued and never
## executed — a scheduled publish is accepted and then simply never happens.
##
## (a) Run the loop inside this app. Right for a single container; wrong the moment
## you run a second replica, because each one would run its own loop.
# APHEX_EMBEDDED_WORKER=true
##
## (b) Drive POST /api/internal/workers/run from outside — platform cron, or the
## bundled loop (`pnpm worker`). Until this secret is set that endpoint 404s, so it
## is never an unauthenticated surface.
##     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# APHEX_WORKER_SECRET=
## Only for the `pnpm worker` loop (defaults shown):
# APHEX_WORKER_URL=http://localhost:5173/api/internal/workers/run
# APHEX_WORKER_INTERVAL_MS=5000

## ── In-admin AI assistant ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
## Set both required values to show the assistant in /admin. Leave either unset
## to keep the feature and POST /api/agent/chat disabled. Omit AGENT_BASE_URL for
## OpenAI, or point it at an OpenAI-compatible endpoint such as OpenRouter or a
## local model router. Local endpoints that ignore auth can use `local` as the key.
# AGENT_API_KEY=
# AGENT_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini
# AGENT_BASE_URL=

## ── Private assets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## Signs /media/:id/:filename URLs so a private asset can be handed to someone with
## no admin session, for a bounded window (`signAssetUrl`). Unset means signing is
## a no-op and verification always fails — private assets stay session-only, which
## is the safe direction.
# APHEX_ASSET_SIGNING_SECRET=

## ── Plugin secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## Encrypts plugin `secret` settings at rest (AES-256-GCM). Keep it stable across
## deploys: rotating it makes existing encrypted values undecryptable. Unset means
## secret fields are read-only rather than stored in plaintext.
##     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# APHEX_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

## ── First-run example content ───────────────────────────────────────────────
## A welcome page and site settings are created the first time the app runs against
## an empty site, so the studio has something in it. Set to false to start bare.
# APHEX_SEED=false

## ── Re-seeding a deployed site ──────────────────────────────────────────────
## POST /api/seed DELETES every page, post and category and writes the demo
## content again. It is open in dev; anywhere else it 404s unless this is set and
## the request carries `Authorization: Bearer <value>`.
##
## Its own variable on purpose. Don't point it at APHEX_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY —
## that key never leaves the server, and this one is sent in a request header on
## every use. Don't share it with the jobs worker secret either: that one is held
## by a cron platform and only runs due jobs, whereas this one erases content.
##     openssl rand -hex 32
# SEED_SECRET=

## ── Dev tunnelling ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## Extra Host headers the dev server will answer to — for exposing localhost via
## cloudflared/ngrok to test webhooks or view the site on a phone.
# DEV_ALLOWED_HOSTS=your-tunnel.trycloudflare.com
